**SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION**

To address the technical limitations of conventional flow measurement systems, particularly in multiphase and non-intrusive fluid applications, the present disclosure provides an improved Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) flowmeter device and a corresponding method for accurate real-time fluid flow measurement.

In accordance with one aspect of the present disclosure, an NMR flowmeter device is provided. The device includes a flow tube (40) defining a fluid passage through which a single-phase or multiphase fluid flows along a primary flow axis. A pre-polarization magnet assembly (42) is disposed circumferentially around or adjacent to a portion of the flow tube (40) to apply a preliminary static magnetic field, thereby aligning the nuclear magnetic moments (spins) of the target fluid nuclei before entering a downstream measurement region.

The downstream measurement region comprises a static magnetic field assembly (48) configured to generate a substantially homogeneous background magnetic field ($B_0$) across the fluid passing therethrough. At least one radio-frequency (RF) excitation coil (46) is positioned relative to the flow tube (40) within the measurement region to transmit RF pulse sequences that tip the pre-aligned nuclear spins. At least one RF receiver coil (47) is arranged to detect resulting nuclear magnetic resonance signals (such as spin-echo or free induction decay signals) induced by the excited nuclei. 

The NMR flowmeter device further includes a integrated processing and control system comprising a control unit (49), a flow velocity determination module (50), a phase fraction analyzer (51), and a data output interface (52). The control unit (49) is operatively coupled to the RF excitation coil (46) and RF receiver coil (47) to synchronize pulse transmission and signal acquisition. The flow velocity determination module (50) processes the received NMR signals to calculate bulk and phase-specific flow velocities based on relaxation and phase-shift phenomena. The phase fraction analyzer (51) processes the amplitude and relaxation spectrum of the detected signals to quantify the relative volumetric phase fractions (e.g., oil, water, gas ratios) of the fluid within the flow tube (40). The calculated flow parameters are subsequently transmitted to external monitoring systems via the data output interface (52).

In accordance with another aspect of the present disclosure, a method for measuring fluid flow utilizing an NMR flowmeter device is provided. The method comprises:
a) conveying a fluid medium through a flow tube (40);  
b) pre-polarizing nuclear spins within the fluid via a pre-polarization magnet assembly (42) located along an upstream section of the flow tube (40);  
c) subjecting the pre-polarized fluid to a magnetic field generated by a static magnetic field assembly (48) in a measurement zone;  
d) applying one or more RF pulse sequences to the fluid via an RF excitation coil (46);  
e) detecting emitted NMR relaxation signals from the fluid via an RF receiver coil (47);  
f) processing the detected NMR signals via a control unit (49) in conjunction with a flow velocity determination module (50) to derive fluid flow velocity;  
g) analyzing the NMR signal characteristics via a phase fraction analyzer (51) to determine the individual volumetric phase fractions of the fluid; and  
h) outputting the derived velocity and phase fraction values through a data output interface (52).

Advantageously, the present device and method allow for continuous, non-invasive, high-accuracy measurement of multiphase fluids without relying on fluid salinity, viscosity, or radioactive sources.
